// whyOwners is why Plutonium has owners where Go has none.
const whyOwners = "Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector: an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly one."

// The explanations of the ownership and lifetime rules (language spec
// section 18). The errors themselves carry explanations specialised to the
// code at fault, built where they are found; these texts are what pluto
// explain prints, and what an error prints without one of its own.

// whyFrame is why a view of the frame cannot outlive it.
const whyFrame = "Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the code."

// whyRace is why Plutonium rejects data races at compile time.
const whyScope = "Go keeps the variables a goroutine uses alive with its garbage collector, however long the goroutine runs. Plutonium has none: a variable lives in its function's frame until its block ends. A sync.Scope waits for the tasks it started before its block ends, so they may use the function's variables, and the compiler checks that nothing they use ends or moves first."

const whyRace = "Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no annotations."

